  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "ff_jpegls_decode_picture",
    "file": "ffmpeg/libavcodec/jpeglsdec.c",
    "description": "Heap buffer overflow is caused by an out-of-bounds memset on the JPEG-LS decoder private state: `memset(state, 0, sizeof(*state));` in `ff_jpegls_decode_picture` writes ~5924 bytes, but `state` points into the generic `MJpegDecodeContext` object when invoked through the AMV/MJPEG path, where that storage is only ~4576 bytes. The root bug is unsafe type punning/casting (`(JpegLSDecodeContext*)s`) without ensuring the actual allocated context is large enough for `JpegLSDecodeContext` before clearing/using `state`.",
    "conditions": "Triggered when the fuzzer drives the AMV/MJPEG decoder into JPEG-LS SOS handling (`s->lossless && s->ls` path in `ff_mjpeg_decode_sos`), which calls `ff_jpegls_decode_picture` with an `MJpegDecodeContext *`. In this PoV, malformed marker data sets JPEG-LS mode and reaches that call even with tiny input; then `ff_jpegls_decode_picture` treats `s` as `JpegLSDecodeContext` and immediately overflows heap on memset. So the required condition is entering JPEG-LS decode with a context allocated for the smaller base MJPEG decode context, not the larger JPEG-LS extended context."
  },
